> pet drops the item about 5 turns later, by that
> time me and my pet are already outside.

I think this highlights one of NetHack's perhaps
unrecognized main attractions that keeps players
returning again and again to the game, while at
the same time debunking all the attempts to remove
ways of playing from the game because they offend
someone's sense of what is "right and proper".

NetHack is fun, I contend, in large part because
of all the ways in which the player can get the
feeling that s/he is _getting away with something_,
"pulling the wool over the DevTeam's eyes", using a
personal hard-won "cheat mode".

Each player can invent/discover a _personal_ set of
"neat tricks I know how to do" that are _so much_
better than "doing stuff the hard way".

Even if those "neat tricks" are patience mode plays,
that to others would be "impossible conducts"
instead, the glory of the game is exactly that one
player can say "combat wombat" == "simplified play"
while another player thinks "combat wombat" ==
"lots of extra sneaky tricks available", and both
are absolutely correct, _for themselves_.

NetHack == "no one right way to play the game", and
that's where a lot of the fun comes into it, it
seems to me.

IMO

